 good work  The Nature Conservancy same thing Nature  Conservancy's assets outstrip the assets  of many African countries in terms of  their land and their holdings who do  they answer to and are all their  decisions in the interest of the country  itself this this is perhaps a scope for  another book or another writer and  another person who's interested in this  but I was interested that this new  generation of diplomats certainly is  passionate about wildlife and the  environment and getting out of embassies  and seeing the reality on the ground  that certainly came out it really jumped  out to other chapters you had you had a  chapter devoted to corruption and the  issue of procurement elections and then  you also had a chapter devoted to  Secretary Clinton during her tenure and  then your conclusions at the end  let me just on those two remaining  chapters ask you in terms of corruption  I would say that definitively there's a  real service that these cables have  performed and have made certainly in  terms of tracking elections shed some  light on that I know myself of having  been an election observer how crucial it  is  to have that information before  observing an election for example I  loved the embassy cables on Elections  because they they describe the  atmospherics behind it as well as the  technical operations of it you know what  constitutes free and fair how do we  measure it what's the yardstick for that  and how do we assess the outcome  embassies don't write just on the day of  the election there's usually several  months of reporting that goes along in  advance because candidates need space in  democracy to operate they need to be  able to advertise they need to have  equal access to media these things are  not a foregone conclusion in a lot of  places in the world but the flavor that  they added and the anecdotes they  described as well when things would go  wrong how local election officials would  work to solve them was interesting and  spoke to human resource fulness and the  reality of elections in places where you  can't count on reliable electricity or  elections taking place in Ukraine in the  middle of winter and people you know  coming on horse-drawn sleighs and things  which is sort of picturesque but what  what do you do when you see one family  member voting for 30 members of his clan  as it happens in Uzbekistan for example  and is considered perfectly normal  because he's here - on behalf of his  clan one man one vote one man one clan  it's a different view and you know the  OSCE doesn't even bother if the  standards are just so different so how  how can you close this gap how can you  suggest ways forward for a country  that's clearly in transition and clearly  has a long way to go  and what meaning do you make from an  election so that when a member of  Congress simply says well was it free  and fair yes or no that doesn't really  get at it you need a lot more context in  order to understand what's happening  iíve seen elections more in Latin  America then in but also in the Czech  Republic and yeah there are a lot of  interesting  differences about all that and it's one  of the more interesting tasks of embassy  officers and people from Washington who  often join us and go out to these  polling places to see how it transpires  on the ground before we conclude I want  to focus on two questions and one  related to the last chapter on Hillary  Clinton and also something that you cite  relevant to Condoleezza Rice and that's  this concept of transform and  transformational diplomacy define what  it is for our viewers and also where is  it now it was something that was  announced by Secretary Rice and the  during secretary Clinton's a term at the  State Department where is it now where  do you see it going what is it and where  is it going I think that was one of  secretary Rice's most fundamental  contributions her two speeches at  Georgetown but the first one in  particular and she cast this in terms  everyone could understand she said look  we have as many American embassy  officers in Germany as we have in India  a country of more than a billion people  how can that be that's hard to argue  with and she began a redeployment that  changed because of Iraq and suddenly  those officers weren't going to India or  to Haiti or any of their they certainly  were going to Germany more but they were  being diverted to Iraq but that idea  that we needed to rethink the globe and  leave the post-war era leave the  post-communist transformation era and  look at the world in a completely  different way was something that she  really keyed off and I think Secretary  Clinton continued along that track  foreign service officers now understand  that it's not likely that they're going  to get a lot of promotions going from  Paris to London to Rome if indeed they  would be allowed to do that which is  highly doubtful the exciting work and  where a lot of the cables come from are  from places that a lot of people have to  look up on the map to find and we're  going to spending more and more time  more energy more commitment and I think  more personnel in those places  because those are the cutting edge of  American foreign policy and American  interests let's conclude on this note  what were your three please I want you  to mention them you articulate it in the  book your three please  at the end your three recommendations  going forward well I I do believe that  the cables ought to be declassified  they're out there on the internet and  what's happened now is that  international students professors  students of diplomacy have access to  them American students if they aspire to  the Foreign Service may feel a little  bit less secure having any kind of  academic deep dive into them and I think  you know the the cats out of the bag  let's just go ahead I also think that we  need professional leadership in public  diplomacy many of the other branches of  the State Department have been  extraordinarily well served by officers  who have come up through the ranks  worked in the field for decades and then  assumed leadership syn undersecretary  roles we've had some excellent political  appointees as well unfortunately public  diplomacy is one cone in which the  turnover has been too rapid the depth of  expertise and the commitment of a lot of  the former under secretaries of public  diplomacy has left the field poorly  served I wish that at some point that  perhaps the next administration might  consider pulling someone from the ranks  of the public diplomacy specialists and  selecting someone in a leadership role  for that mary i want to say you've  written an excellent book i am gonna put  it up at this time it really is very  thoughtful thank you any student a  foreign policy and practitioner of  foreign policy it's not only informative  but it really is fascinating i learned a  number of things that even though i've  had 25 years in government that i  learned from this book and really just  thank you so much for your contribution  well thank you secretary it's been fun  chatting with you thank you  likewise
 

